WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:38.040
You know, when a computer starts up, it has
to boot – bootstrap, is what that means.

00:00:38.040 --> 00:00:43.540
And that boot straps itself by continuing
to engage in more and more complex processes

00:00:43.540 --> 00:00:44.630
as it starts up.

00:00:44.630 --> 00:00:48.860
So, it starts up with a simple process, and
that triggers a more complex process.

00:00:48.860 --> 00:00:53.040
And that triggers a more complex process,
and that way, the computer boots itself into

00:00:53.040 --> 00:00:54.040
existence.

00:00:54.040 --> 00:00:58.040
Well, that’s exactly what we did, except
we did it over like three billion years, right?

00:00:58.040 --> 00:01:02.610
We started out as these unbelievably simple
organisms that can pretty much do nothing

00:01:02.610 --> 00:01:09.860
at all, except replicate and develop much
more and more complex forms over this tremendously

00:01:09.860 --> 00:01:12.280
long period of evolutionary history, right?

00:01:12.280 --> 00:01:17.000
So, that’s how we solved the problem that
you can’t know anything without knowing

00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:20.440
something, and that you can’t have knowledge
without generating it.

00:01:20.440 --> 00:01:22.430
It’s: A little knowledge, a little more
generation.

00:01:22.430 --> 00:01:24.060
A little knowledge, a little more generation.

00:01:24.060 --> 00:01:29.640
A little knowledge-- this huge spiraling process
that expends-- extends over vast amounts of

00:01:29.640 --> 00:01:35.380
time; information encoded in your body, right,
and as part of your-- as part of the nature

00:01:35.380 --> 00:01:40.080
of your being; and information encoded in
your culture, reflected inside, you enacted

00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:46.520
out in the world.

00:01:46.520 --> 00:01:53.630
And as scientists, you know, with this 500-year
history of science behind us, we always believe

00:01:53.630 --> 00:01:57.590
that it’s the material substrate of things
that’s the reality.

00:01:57.590 --> 00:02:02.330
But it is more complicated than that, because
even the material substrate that we consider

00:02:02.330 --> 00:02:07.000
as scientists isn’t merely unformed stuff,
right?

00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:08.240
It has structure.

00:02:08.240 --> 00:02:09.240
It’s patterned.

00:02:09.240 --> 00:02:15.650
It’s full of information, and there are
physicists working now who believe that conceptualization

00:02:15.650 --> 00:02:20.560
of the ground of being – the material ground
uh, of being – as information is a more

00:02:20.560 --> 00:02:26.140
fruitful metaphor than conceptualization of
the ground of being as like unformed matter.

00:02:26.140 --> 00:02:28.100
It’s not unformed, right?

00:02:28.100 --> 00:02:30.070
It’s patterned and regular.

00:02:30.070 --> 00:02:35.680
Informative so that if you investigate it,
it reveals order as not just a material structure.

00:02:35.680 --> 00:02:40.580
So, if you go back to Democratus, right, the
person who originated the atomic hypothesis,

00:02:40.580 --> 00:02:43.560
Democratus actually says two things – not
one.

00:02:43.560 --> 00:02:47.080
He says: Everything’s made out of atoms
– little bits of stuff.

00:02:47.080 --> 00:02:52.849
But the other thing he says is: Atoms array
themselves in space array themselves-- And

00:02:52.849 --> 00:02:57.959
what that means is that the atomic structure
of things is patterned, and that the pattern

00:02:57.959 --> 00:03:00.590
is just as real as the atoms themselves.

00:03:00.590 --> 00:03:02.360
It’s the pattern that’s the knowledge,
right?

00:03:02.360 --> 00:03:07.190
It’s the pattern that’s the information.

00:03:07.190 --> 00:03:15.940
So, what we encounter as conscious beings
is this complexly patterned array, which we

00:03:15.940 --> 00:03:18.520
then turn into knowledge – useable knowledge.

00:03:18.520 --> 00:03:22.680
I said: Well, how would this metaphorical
representation work?

00:03:22.680 --> 00:03:27.260
And so uh, the way I want you to look at this
figure is like this: Imagine this as distant,

00:03:27.260 --> 00:03:29.110
right, as lurking in the background.

00:03:29.110 --> 00:03:36.600
So, this is the ground of all being manifesting
itself as one primordial archetype, or one,

00:03:36.600 --> 00:03:41.849
one metaph-- one standard motive metaphorical
representation.

00:03:41.849 --> 00:03:42.849
Why this figure?

00:03:42.849 --> 00:03:46.379
Well, this is complicated, I think.

00:03:46.379 --> 00:03:53.000
If you show men a picture of a beautiful woman
with her eyes averted, while you’re doing

00:03:53.000 --> 00:03:58.980
a brain scan mapping of their nucleus accumbens,
nothing happens.

00:03:58.980 --> 00:04:00.190
Why is that relevant?

00:04:00.190 --> 00:04:04.849
Well, the nucleus accumbens is part of the
underlying emotional circuitry that governs

00:04:04.849 --> 00:04:07.250
approach behaviour and pleasure.

00:04:07.250 --> 00:04:10.239
And approach behaviour and pleasure are very
tightly intertwined.

00:04:10.239 --> 00:04:15.340
If you show the same man a picture of a beautiful
woman with her eyes locked on his, his nucleus

00:04:15.340 --> 00:04:16.910
accumbens will light up.

00:04:16.910 --> 00:04:18.019
Why is that?

00:04:18.019 --> 00:04:24.240
Well, it’s partly because men are innately
in-- attuned to hu-- to female beauty.

00:04:24.240 --> 00:04:25.849
Female beauty has a standard form.

00:04:25.849 --> 00:04:27.780
It’s replicable cross-culturally.

00:04:27.780 --> 00:04:31.290
It constitutes the average human…

00:04:31.290 --> 00:04:32.620
human female form.

00:04:32.620 --> 00:04:37.840
But more than that, the gaze-locking is an
indication of shared attention.

00:04:37.840 --> 00:04:42.180
And it’s also the indication of the initial
establishment of a shared attentional space.

00:04:42.180 --> 00:04:46.690
And it’s an invitation, and that invitation
activates approach circuitry, even if it’s

00:04:46.690 --> 00:04:47.690
just a picture.

00:04:47.690 --> 00:04:52.169
So, and you can see this if you walk into
any drug store, or any-any s-- any drug store

00:04:52.169 --> 00:04:53.500
that sells magazines.

00:04:53.500 --> 00:04:54.500
What do you see?

00:04:54.500 --> 00:04:58.560
Well, in the men’s magazines, you see an
infinite array of beautiful women.

00:04:58.560 --> 00:05:03.260
And in the female magazines, the women’s
magazines, you see an infinite array of beautiful

00:05:03.260 --> 00:05:04.260
women.

00:05:04.260 --> 00:05:05.810
Now, just exactly why is that?

00:05:05.810 --> 00:05:09.740
Well, there’s something absolutely compelling
about female beauty.

00:05:09.740 --> 00:05:13.450
And then, you have to ask yourself: What the
hell does compelling mean?

00:05:13.450 --> 00:05:20.440
OK, so what compelling means is: You’re
busily engaged in a goal-directed task and

00:05:20.440 --> 00:05:25.850
something happens in your peripheral vision,
so to speak, that attracts your attention.

00:05:25.850 --> 00:05:30.720
Now, that attention is attracted by processes
that are fundamentally unconscious; which

00:05:30.720 --> 00:05:34.170
means, processes that occur before you can
think.

00:05:34.170 --> 00:05:42.590
So, you can imagine a loose collection of
college-aged males having a conversation in

00:05:42.590 --> 00:05:45.669
a bar, when someone beautiful walks by.

00:05:45.669 --> 00:05:50.279
And one or more of them catch her out of the
corner of their eyes.

00:05:50.279 --> 00:05:52.010
Orientation, right?

00:05:52.010 --> 00:05:53.010
Unconscious.

00:05:53.010 --> 00:05:54.010
Why?

00:05:54.010 --> 00:05:58.870
Because there’s something about that pattern
form that grips attentional systems and directs

00:05:58.870 --> 00:06:05.600
observation towards it.

00:06:05.600 --> 00:06:07.050
So then, you think: OK.

00:06:07.050 --> 00:06:10.950
Human beings are really, really complicated
pattern processors.

00:06:10.950 --> 00:06:14.940
And you think: Well, partly what we’re trying
to get a handle on here is the nature of the

00:06:14.940 --> 00:06:15.940
world.

00:06:15.940 --> 00:06:19.669
And partly what we know already is that there
are aspects of the world that you can’t

00:06:19.669 --> 00:06:21.630
understand always.

00:06:21.630 --> 00:06:26.120
And then, you think: Well, people are trying
to get a grip on the fact that there are parts

00:06:26.120 --> 00:06:27.420
of the world that you can’t understand.

00:06:27.420 --> 00:06:32.900
So, that there’s this transcendant element
to being that always escapes encapsulation.

00:06:32.900 --> 00:06:34.510
How would you represent it?

00:06:34.510 --> 00:06:40.469
Well, it’s the transcendent element of things
that always attracts your attention, implicitly,

00:06:40.469 --> 00:06:41.729
beyond your control.

00:06:41.729 --> 00:06:49.770
So, loud noise or a scream, or the cry of
a baby or anything horrific, right?

00:06:49.770 --> 00:06:54.440
Blood, broken bodies…

00:06:54.440 --> 00:07:01.810
These are stimuli that are so representative
of trouble that you can’t help but attend

00:07:01.810 --> 00:07:03.159
to them.

00:07:03.159 --> 00:07:08.820
And then, you can imagine that all the stimuli,
so to speak, that you can’t help but attend

00:07:08.820 --> 00:07:16.570
to, can be amalgamated into representations
of… the transcendent aspect of reality.

00:07:16.570 --> 00:07:19.900
And that’s what you see in this representation
right here.

00:07:19.900 --> 00:07:29.240
So, you see a weird intermingling of female
sexuality, plus some very distinct genital

00:07:29.240 --> 00:07:30.240
symbolism.

00:07:30.240 --> 00:07:31.240
Why genital?

00:07:31.240 --> 00:07:36.140
So, imagine that that’s a vulval opening.

00:07:36.140 --> 00:07:37.140
Why?

00:07:37.140 --> 00:07:41.530
Before we had any scientific knowledge at
all-- let’s go 10,000 years ago.

00:07:41.530 --> 00:07:43.440
What the hell’s inside a body?

00:07:43.440 --> 00:07:46.500
Well, we knew that, I guess, a little bit
